    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (36 tests, 0 failures) with a green build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. Added a lookup of country calling codes with their required national-number length:\n```java\nprivate static final Map<String, Integer> COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTH = Map.of(\n    \"61\", 9,\n    \"1\", 10);\n```\n\n2. Extended `normalizeTelephone(...)`, after the existing E.164 shape check, to strip the `+`, match the leading country code, and reject with a 400 (`InvalidRequestException`) when the remaining national digits don't match the required length for that country:\n```java\nString digits = candidate.substring(1);\nfor (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTH.entrySet()) {\n    String countryCode = entry.getKey();\n    if (digits.startsWith(countryCode)) {\n        int nationalLength = digits.length() - countryCode.length();\n        if (nationalLength != entry.getValue()) {\n            throw new InvalidRequestException(List.of(\"telephone\"));\n        }\n        break;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe two known country codes (`61` and `1`) never collide as prefixes, so iteration order is safe. Numbers with unknown country codes (e.g. `+64`) are unaffected and keep passing. The new `Cp26Tests` case (`+61 123` \u2192 400, since `+61` requires 9 national digits) passes, along with all existing telephone tests (`+61412345678` and `+6421123456` still accepted).",
